<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ouster_smoke.sdf - smoke / dust obscuration demo for gz_sensors_ouster.

    ros2 launch gz_sensors_ouster ouster_standalone.launch.py \
      world:=ouster_smoke.sdf

  Every cloud in this world is an ordinary Gazebo <particle_emitter>. The
  plugin mirrors them automatically (<particle_obscuration> defaults to true),
  so the smoke you SEE in the GUI is the smoke the LiDAR SCANS - no plugin
  configuration is needed to reproduce anything below. See
  docs/MODEL_REFERENCES.md 11 for the physics and README.md for the knobs,
  including the <obscurant> block for authored volumes with an explicit
  extinction coefficient.

  Toggle any cloud at runtime and watch the point cloud change:

    gz topic -t /model/A_smoke_100/link/link/particle_emitter/emitter/cmd \
      -m gz.msgs.ParticleEmitter -p 'emitting: {data: false}'

  Raycast mode only - obscuration is integrated along each cast beam, and the
  panels pipeline has no equivalent path. Like the other raycast worlds this
  one loads NO rendering system, so it runs headless anywhere.

  Layout: everything sits on azimuth arcs around the sensor at the origin.
  Walls are at 14 m, clouds at 7 m. Each emitter's <size> is
  1.2x1.2x3.0 m, dilated by particle_growth x
  v_mean x lifetime = 0.3 m on every side, so a beam through the middle of a
  cloud crosses 1.8 m of it starting at 6.1 m.

  ZONE A (0-64 deg) - density ladder, identical 0.8-retro walls behind
    clouds of rising <particle_scatter_ratio>. With the default
    <particle_extinction> of 1.0 /m per unit ratio:

      ratio 0.05 -> sigma 0.05/m, tau 0.09, wall rho 0.6682, smoke rho 0.0019  => reports the wall
      ratio 0.15 -> sigma 0.15/m, tau 0.27, wall rho 0.4662, smoke rho 0.0057  => reports the wall
      ratio 0.35 -> sigma 0.35/m, tau 0.63, wall rho 0.2269, smoke rho 0.0132  => reports the wall
      ratio 0.65 -> sigma 0.65/m, tau 1.17, wall rho 0.0771, smoke rho 0.0245  => reports the smoke
      ratio 1.0  -> sigma 1.00/m, tau 1.80, wall rho 0.0219, smoke rho 0.0377  => reports the smoke

    So the first three rungs show the wall surviving and dimming, and the last
    two show the smoke itself becoming the strongest return - the transition a
    real lidar makes as smoke thickens.

  ZONE B (88-142 deg) - emitter shape gallery: box, cylinder, ellipsoid and
    point emitters at one density. Point emitters ignore <size> in Gazebo, so
    the plugin models them as the ball the particles travel through.

  ZONE C (166-206 deg) - smoke against open sky, no wall behind. These beams
    would be misses in clear air; the returns they produce come from the
    medium itself, which is how a phantom obstacle appears in real smoke.

  ZONE D (230-262 deg) - NEAR_IR airlight. A 0.05-retro and a 0.95-retro wall
    behind identical clouds, plus an unobscured 0.95 wall for reference. Smoke
    BRIGHTENS the ambient channel toward albedo x illumination (0.8) while it
    DARKENS the laser channels - the signature of fog/smoke on a real Ouster.
    The dark wall disappears completely and the bright one survives: same
    smoke, opposite outcome, because extinction acts on the target's own
    reflectance.

  ZONE E (290-330 deg) - clear-air control walls at three reflectances, for an
    in-frame A/B against the obscured zones.

  Zone spacing is deliberate: a cloud subtends about +/-8 deg from the sensor,
  so the arcs are set far enough apart that each rung obscures ONLY its own
  wall. Move one and the ladder stops measuring what its table says.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="ouster_smoke">

    <physics name="default" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.004</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-altimeter-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Altimeter"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Imu"/>
    <!-- Serves each emitter's .../cmd topic so the clouds can be toggled and
         retuned at runtime; the plugin re-reads them every scan. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-particle-emitter-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::ParticleEmitter"/>

    <scene>
      <ambient>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</ambient>
      <background>0.7 0.8 0.9 1</background>
    </scene>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.5 0.3 -1</direction>
    </light>

    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>80 80</size></plane></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>80 80</size></plane></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient><diffuse>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ZONE A: density ladder (see the header table). -->
    <model name="A_wall_005">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14.000 0.000 1.50 0 0 0.0000</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.2 1.80 4.00</size></box></geometry>
          <laser_retro>0.8</laser_retro>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.80 0.80 0.80 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.80 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>
    <model name="A_smoke_005">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>7.000 0.000 1.20 0 0 0.0000</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <particle_emitter name="emitter" type="box">
          <emitting>true</emitting>
          <size>1.2 1.2 3.0</size>
          <particle_size>0.6 0.6 0.6</particle_size>
          <lifetime>2.0</lifetime>
          <min_velocity>0.1</min_velocity>
          <max_velocity>0.2</max_velocity>
          <rate>40</rate>
          <scale_rate>0.4</scale_rate>
          <particle_scatter_ratio>0.05</particle_scatter_ratio>
          <material>
            <diffuse>0.6 0.6 0.6</diffuse>
            <specular>0.6 0.6 0.6</specular>
          </material>
        </particle_emitter>
      </link>
    </model>
